+---
+node: record-store
+---
+
+# Decision: Whole-Visit Revisions for Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+Two field workers can edit the same plot visit independently, days apart, while tablets are offline. The server receives whole visit states and must decide what the visit's current state is, and which submissions a coordinator has to look at before that visit can be exported to the science partner.
+
+Two failures are at stake. The obvious one is losing a submitted edit: the team's current spreadsheet import overwrites corrections without showing which submission was lost. The subtler one is a server that combines two edits and combines two observations that concern the same species entry and its cover estimate — nobody sees that combination happen, and the partner receives it. A visit may sit outside one week's file while a coordinator judges it; that is a cost the team can see and absorb. A wrong combination in the partner's file is neither visible nor absorbable.
+
+The team accepted the first cost deliberately: a larger coordinator review queue, operated by two people, in exchange for the server never judging whether two edits are scientifically related.
+
+Stakes: the record store's submission contract, the local state a tablet keeps while offline, and the shape of the Friday export all follow from this choice.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Gates
+
+- **No combination without a human.** The mechanism must not require the server to know which scientific fields of a visit are related. Whole-visit revisions passes. Field-aware three-way merge is eliminated: it needs maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, and such a rule is a judgement about scientific relatedness that the server applies without a coordinator seeing it.
+- **One submission determines an exported visit.** The visit state that reaches the partner must be one stored submission, not a composition of several. Inherited from the tenet "Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable". Whole-visit revisions passes. Field-aware three-way merge is eliminated: its current state is composed from more than one submission.
+
+### Weighted Criteria
+
+- **Coordinator review volume** (major): how many submissions need a coordinator's judgement before a visit can be exported.
+- **Readiness of the weekly export** (major): how often a delivery is missing visits that are waiting on review.
+- **Scientific risk of a silent combination** (major): the chance that two edits get combined in a way the team would have wanted to judge together, with the combination reaching the partner unnoticed. Derived from the tenet "Do not silently discard a submitted edit".
+- **Maintenance burden** (major): the rules the mechanism needs as the visit schema and field practice change.
+- **Tablet complexity while offline** (moderate): the local state a tablet must keep for the server to interpret an edit made without connectivity.
+- **Asymmetry of harm** (moderate): which failure is worse — a visit missing from one delivery, or a wrong combination delivered to the science partner.
+- **Reversibility** (moderate): how hard it is to recover if the mechanism turns out to be wrong.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+Each submission is a complete visit state that names the revision it was derived from. A submission naming the visit's current revision advances the visit. A submission naming any earlier revision of that visit is held as a competing revision. A coordinator compares the competing whole visits, selects one, and the other stays in the visit's history with its author and outcome. The server never looks inside a payload to decide anything, and never combines two submissions.
+
+*Demonstration.* Plot 41-B, 3 August. Worker A revises the species identification for *Ammophila arenaria* and its cover estimate. Worker B, working from the same base revision a week earlier, adds a species entry and corrects an observer name. Both submissions are whole visits. The store holds A's submission as the current revision and B's as a pending revision. The coordinator sees two complete visits side by side and selects one. If the species entry from B should survive alongside A's revision, that requires a further submission rather than a combination — the clearest cost of this option.
+
+*Analysis.*
+
+- No combination without a human: **passes** — the store never compares fields of two revisions.
+- One submission determines an exported visit: **passes** — the current revision is one stored payload.
+- Coordinator review volume: **weak** — every edit derived from a stale base becomes coordinator work, including edits that touch unrelated fields.
+- Readiness of the weekly export: **weak** — a visit with a pending revision is held out of the export until it is resolved.
+- Scientific risk of a silent combination: **strong** — no combination happens at all.
+- Maintenance burden: **strong** — nothing beyond the payload schema and the base-revision rule must be kept current.
+- Tablet complexity while offline: **strong** — the tablet keeps one base revision ID per visit and sends complete visit states; it never computes field-level changes offline.
+- Asymmetry of harm: **strong** — a missing visit is visible and recoverable, since the visit is exported once resolved; a wrong combination delivered to the partner is not.
+- Reversibility: **strong** — every submitted revision is retained, so a later combination can be computed from stored submissions and a bad selection can be resolved again.
+
+*Summary.* The cost is paid in coordinator time; the correctness risk is paid nowhere.
+
+### Field-Aware Three-Way Merge
+
+Each submission supplies the base state it was derived from and the changes made to it. The server compares the changes field by field and combines changes that touch different fields. Overlapping changes — the same field, the same species entry, the same observer — go to a coordinator. The mechanism needs maintained rules that say what counts as the same unit of change: whether the observer list is compared as a set or a sequence, whether two species entries naming the same taxon are one entry or two, and whether a species identification and its cover estimate are one change or two.
+
+*Demonstration.* With rules that treat the observer list as a set and species entries as keyed by taxon, worker B's added species and corrected observer name combine automatically with worker A's revised cover estimate, and the visit becomes export-ready with no coordinator involved. With a rule that treats a species identification and its cover estimate as one unit of change, the same two submissions land in a coordinator's queue instead — the rule decides the outcome, not the coordinator.
+
+*Analysis.*
+
+- No combination without a human: **eliminated** — the rules must decide which fields are related, and a rule that is wrong about relatedness combines two observations without anyone noticing.
+- One submission determines an exported visit: **eliminated** — the current state is composed from more than one submission.
+- Coordinator review volume: **strong** — disjoint edits combine automatically, so only overlaps reach a coordinator.
+- Readiness of the weekly export: **strong** — visits whose edits touch different fields are export-ready immediately.
+- Scientific risk of a silent combination: **weak** — a rule that pairs a species identification with its cover estimate, or that treats two differently-worded species entries as the same entry, silently produces a visit nobody reviewed.
+- Maintenance burden: **weak** — rules must be revised whenever the visit schema or field practice changes, and a missing rule degrades quietly rather than loudly.
+- Tablet complexity while offline: **weak** — the tablet must retain base snapshots and compute per-field change sets offline, including deciding which entries are the same unit of change.
+- Asymmetry of harm: **weak** — a wrong combination can reach the partner's file with no visible trace.
+- Reversibility: **weak** — once a combined state has been exported, the partner holds the combined values, and separating them afterwards is manual work.
+
+*Summary.* Fewer coordinator hours and faster deliveries, bought with rules that encode scientific relatedness and that fail quietly.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions. It passes both gates and is strongly favoured on scientific risk, maintenance burden, tablet complexity, asymmetry of harm, and reversibility. It is weakly fitted on coordinator review volume and export readiness, and that is the trade the team accepted: a bigger queue worked by two coordinators rather than merge rules that can combine scientifically related edits without anyone noticing.
+
+The decision turns on the tenet "Prefer a larger coordinator review queue over a server that recombines scientifically related edits on its own", and it upholds the tenets "Do not silently discard a submitted edit" and "Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable".
+
+What it forecloses: a visit is never export-ready on its own once its edits were derived from a stale base, and a coordinator cannot combine two submitted visits — selecting one of them is the resolution the contract offers. Both costs are bounded by the fact that every submitted revision is retained.
+
+### What tablet sync must accommodate
+
+- **One base revision ID per visit.** The tablet keeps the revision ID its local copy of a visit was derived from and sends it with every submission. Without it the server cannot tell a fresh edit from a competing one.
+- **Complete visit states on the wire.** A submission carries the whole visit, not a set of changed fields. The tablet's local edits therefore accumulate into one current local visit state.
+- **Three server outcomes, three tablet behaviours.** A submission accepted as the current revision becomes the tablet's new base. A submission held for review is shown as sent for review and does not become the tablet's base; the tablet keeps its own state and awaits the outcome. A rejected submission is shown with its reason, and a rejection caused by an idempotency conflict needs a fresh key and an explicit retry.
+- **One submission per visit per disconnection.** Because a submission carries the whole visit, several offline edits to one visit collapse into the latest local state, submitted once against the base the tablet started from.
+- **Idempotent retries.** The key for an attempt is reused unchanged when the tablet retries after a connection failure, so a dropped response does not create a second edit.
+- **Pending status stays visible.** A visit the server is holding for review is shown to its submitter as under review, since the tablet's local copy is no longer the current state.
+
+### What review and export must accommodate
+
+- **A comparison screen, not a diff of fields.** The coordinator compares two or more complete visit states side by side, because the choice being made is between whole observations. The screen shows each candidate's author, receipt time, and the revision it was written against.
+- **A queue that reflects what is pending.** The store's unresolved-work listing is the queue, ordered so the oldest pending revision surfaces first. A visit's pending revisions are one review task: one resolution closes them all.
+- **A resolution that records its reasoning by reference.** The coordinator selects one pending revision or none; the store retains the coordinator's identity, the selected revision or its absence, and the outcome of every contributing revision, so the decision can be explained later without reconstructing it.
+- **Visits under review are absent from the delivery, not stale.** A visit with unresolved review work is held out of the partner's file entirely. It is not exported as its last accepted revision: that would deliver a value the team has already decided not to stand behind.
+- **Traceability to the revision.** Each exported row comes from one current revision and carries its revision ID, author, and receipt time, so an exported value can be traced back to the submission that produced it.
+
+## When to Revisit This Decision
+
+The choice remains live while any of these conditions holds. They are worth checking at the end of a season rather than assumed either way.
+
+- The coordinator queue regularly exceeds what two coordinators can clear before a Friday delivery, and visits waiting on review repeatedly miss a delivery.
+- The science partner asks for per-field provenance of exported values, or begins asking why a given value changed.
+- Visit payloads grow — species entries per visit — far enough that resending a whole visit per edit becomes a material cost on tablets that may be offline for two days.
+- Field practice starts producing edits that routinely touch the same species entry from different tablets, so that the queue fills with cases whose two submissions differ in unrelated ways.
+- A maintained rule emerges that the team trusts to identify which edits are scientifically related. At that point the gate this decision set could be reopened on its own terms.
+
+Because the store retains every submitted revision with its payload, author, base revision, and receipt time, a merge mechanism could later be added over the same stored data without migrating history. The revisitable object is the acceptance rule, not the retained data — and a merge added later would arrive with the same scientific risk this decision was taken to avoid, unless the rule that identifies related edits is one the team trusts.
